Let me look at the `rans_uncompress_to_4x16` function to see if it can cause a double-free or heap corruption. But actually, let me step back and think about the actual vulnerability more carefully.

The crash is a heap-double-free in `decode_names`. Looking at the code, the `free_context` function frees all `desc[i].buf` for `i < max_tok*16`. The double-free would occur if two `desc[i].buf` pointers are the same.

Let me look at the copy path once more very carefully:

```c
if (ttype & 64) {
    if (o+2 >= sz) goto err;
    int j = in[o++]<<4;
    j += in[o++];
    if (ttype & 128) {
        tnum++;
        if (tnum >= MAX_TOKENS)
            goto err;
        ctx->max_tok = tnum+1;
        memset(&ctx->desc[tnum<<4], 0, 16*sizeof(ctx->desc[tnum]));
    }

    if ((ttype & 15) != 0 && (ttype & 128)) {
        ctx->desc[tnum<<4].buf = malloc(nreads);
        ...
    }

    i = (tnum<<4) | (ttype&15);
    if (j >= i)
        goto err;

    ctx->desc[i].buf_l = 0;
    ctx->desc[i].buf_a = ctx->desc[j].buf_a;
    if (ctx->desc[i].buf) free(ctx->desc[i].buf);
    ctx->desc[i].buf = malloc(ctx->desc[i].buf_a);
    if (!ctx->desc[i].buf)
        goto err;

    memcpy(ctx->desc[i].buf, ctx->desc[j].buf, ctx->desc[i].buf_a);
    continue;
}
```

Wait, I need to check: what if `ttype&15 == 0` and `ttype&128` is set? Then `i = tnum<<4`. In the second block, `(ttype & 15) != 0` is false, so `desc[tnum<<4].buf` is NOT allocated (it was zeroed by memset). Then `desc[i].buf` is NULL, `free(NULL)` is no-op, and `desc[i].buf = malloc(...)`. Fine.

What if `ttype&15 != 0` and `ttype&128` is set? Then `desc[tnum<<4].buf` is allocated. `i = (tnum<<4) | (ttype&15) != tnum<<4`. So `desc[i].buf` is a different slot. Fine.

What if `ttype&64` is set but `ttype&128` is NOT set? Then `tnum` is not incremented, and `desc[tnum<<4]` is not zeroed. `i = (tnum<<4) | (ttype&15)`. `desc[i].buf` is freed and re-allocated. Fine.

Hmm, so where's the double-free? Let me look at the "Load compressed block" path again:

```c
if ((ttype & 15) != 0 && (ttype & 128)) {
    if (ctx->desc[tnum<<4].buf) free(ctx->desc[tnum<<4].buf);
    ctx->desc[tnum<<4].buf = malloc(nreads);
    ...
}

int64_t clen, ulen = uncompressed_size(&in[o], sz-o);
if (ulen < 0 || ulen >= INT_MAX)
    goto err;
i = (tnum<<4) | (ttype&15);

if (i >= MAX_TBLOCKS || i < 0)
    goto err;

ctx->desc[i].buf_l = 0;
if (ctx->desc[i].buf) free(ctx->desc[i].buf);
ctx->desc[i].buf = malloc(ulen);
```

Wait, here's a potential issue. If `ttype&15 == 0` and `ttype&128` is set, then `desc[tnum<<4].buf` is NOT allocated in the first block (since `ttype&15 == 0`). Then `i = tnum<<4`. `desc[i].buf` is freed (NULL, no-op) and re-allocated. Fine.

But what if `ttype&15 != 0` and `ttype&128` is set? Then `desc[tnum<<4].buf` is allocated. `i = (tnum<<4) | (ttype&15) != tnum<<4`. So `desc[i].buf` is a different slot. Fine.

