Let me look at the `decode_token_type` function:
```c
static enum name_type decode_token_type(name_context *ctx, int ntok) {
    int id = ntok<<4;
    if (ctx->desc[id].buf_l >= ctx->desc[id].buf_a) return -1;
    return ctx->desc[id].buf[ctx->desc[id].buf_l++];
}
```

This reads from `desc[ntok<<4].buf`. If `desc[ntok<<4].buf` is NULL but `buf_l < buf_a`, this would be a NULL deref. But that's not a double-free.

Let me think about this from a different angle. The double-free in `free_context` happens when two `desc[i].buf` pointers are the same. The only way this can happen is if the same allocation is assigned to two different `desc[i].buf` slots.

Looking at the copy path:
```c
ctx->desc[i].buf = malloc(ctx->desc[i].buf_a);
memcpy(ctx->desc[i].buf, ctx->desc[j].buf, ctx->desc[i].buf_a);
```

This is a fresh malloc, so `desc[i].buf` is unique. Unless `malloc` returns the same pointer as `desc[j].buf`... which can't happen since `desc[j].buf` is still allocated.

